NNAAMMEE
     ttss — timestamp input

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ttss [--ii | --ss] [--mm] [_f_o_r_m_a_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttss utility prepends a timestamp to each line of standard input and
     writes it to standard output.

     The options are as follows:

     --ii      Display time elapsed since the last timestamp.

     --mm      Display timestamps derived from a strictly linearly increasing
             clock.  Without --mm, timestamps reflect the current date and time,
             including time jumps if the system time is changed.

     --ss      Display time elapsed since the start of the program.

     The optional _f_o_r_m_a_t argument controls how the timestamp is displayed, ac‐
     cording to the conversion specifications described in the strftime(3)
     manual page.  The default format is "%b %d %H:%M:%S"; or "%H:%M:%S" if
     one of the --ii or --ss options is used.

     Some additional conversion specifications are also supported to append
     microsecond resolution: %%..SS, %%..ss, and %%..TT; which are similar to %%SS, %%ss,
     and %%TT.  Examples: "10.00001", "1656427781.00001", and "4:20:00.00001".

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
           $ (echo foo; sleep 2; echo bar) | ts
           Jun 28 12:13:38 foo
           Jun 28 12:13:40 bar

           $ ls | ts -i %.S
           00.000452 CVS
           00.000595 Makefile
           00.000004 ts.1
           00.000004 ts.c

